{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790337900000-architect-dao-stream-serve-q1-q7-mob-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 12:14:42 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 19:14:42 +07\n     3\t\n     4\t# dao.stream.serve Q1–Q7 — round 2 cross-read (GLM-5.3)\n     5\t\n     6\tI read codex's round-1 answer verbatim (`collab/1790337700000-…mob-r1.gpt-6-sol.findings.md`) and opened every line it cites. Codex's citations check out where they point (two are off by a line or two but land on the right passages; noted inline). One of its claims moved me (Q5's OD-1 write-effect defect — I missed it in round 1 and say so below); the rest I hold or adopt on evidence.\n     7\t\n     8\t---\n     9\t\n    10\t## Q3 — HOLD (opaque ids now), adopting one of codex's concerns\n    11\t\n    12\tCodex: self-certifying from the start with proof of control at registration, because \"an open relay routing by string equality can bind that name to the wrong registrant\" (citing `dao.stream.discovery.md:70` — verified: mechanism 1; and design:224, which lands in §7.4's rendezvous text — the register/relay passage is §7.3 at 226; substance unaffected).\n    13\t\n    14\tThe decisive fact codex's fix cannot answer: **the thing a dialer actually trusts — the logical-stream identity — is unauthenticated at this layer no matter what the peer id proves.** The contract records this itself: \"Identity is an opaque value nothing authenticates… Self-certifying identities and signed or hash-chained elements live in the payload, above the stream\" (`dao.stream.md:1087-1092`). With proof-of-control registration, an attacker who knows a descriptor's candidates can still serve a **fake stream under the real identity**; catching that requires kickoff-hash streams or the Noise-shaped channel — both correctly deferred (design §12, findings.md:284). So registration-proof closes one narrow attack (peer-id squatting on a shared relay) while leaving the adjacent, larger one (fake-stream impersonation) fully open: it is neither needed in the design's stated threat model (dev-only, trusted networks, findings.md:284) nor sufficient outside it. Also decisive: v1 peer ids are **composition-assigned** (findings.md:284); with unguessable assigned ids, squatting requires descriptor knowledge, which is the capability being protected. Relay routing stays string equality under either scheme (findings.md:226).\n    15\t\n    16\tWhat I adopt from codex: the concern is real and must be specified — duplicate/contested `:serve/register` must be defined relay behavior (refuse/evict as composition policy), which I flagged in round 1 and fold into Q4's policy seam. Named tripwire: **if a relay is exposed to non-trusted peers during v1's lifetime, the required work is channel authentication (Noise-shaped channel) and self-certifying stream identity — not registration-proof alone**; at that point my position flips. HOLD.\n    17\t\n    18\t## Q4 — CHANGE (adopt codex's formulation)\n    19\t\n    20\tCodex: composition-supplied relay admission policy, open admission only as the default of explicitly local/trusted deployments; postage a possible policy, not a settled protocol answer (citing findings.md:224/§7.3, `dao.stream.discovery.md:159` — verified: postage \"Accepted\" in principle — and `dao.stream.md:285` — verified: descriptors carry no authorization).\n    21\t\n    22\tI verified the load-bearing distinction and it is sound: the contract's no-gating rule governs **descriptors and `attach!`** (`dao.stream.md:285-292`); `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial` are relay-control frames the **serve layer** owns (findings.md:43), so an admission policy there contradicts nothing. Codex's shape is also more invariant-aligned than Fable's flat \"v1 accepts any\": policy-as-composition-data with an open default keeps the reference relay's dev behavior identical while refusing to bake \"accept anyone\" into the spec as a protocol property — and it subsumes my round-1 bounded-state flag (link/peer-table lifecycle, caps — otherwise criterion 5, findings.md:292, passes while the relay leaks links). Postage stays a future policy, consistent with `dao.stream.discovery.md:231-233` (design unspecified). CHANGE: my round-1 position plus codex's seam, merged.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Q5 — CHANGE (synthesis; codex found a real defect in OD-1's drafting, and its read-truth concern resolves declaratively)\n    25\t\n    26\tCodex: accept OD-3(a)+(2) and OD-2, revise OD-1 separately, resolve proxy read truth first. Three sub-points, checked individually:\n    27\t\n    28\t1. \"`:retry?` needs no OD-1\" — TRUE and I said the same in round 1 (open maps, `dao.stream.md:109-112`). Not load-bearing either way.\n    29\t2. **OD-1's \"nothing appended\" default is unsafe under OD-2 — TRUE, and I missed it.** The proposed rule reads \"treats it as: not `ok`; nothing observed and **nothing appended**; and not retryable\" (`dao.stream.md:836-838`), while OD-2's accepted semantics make an append's effect *unknown* unless clean failure is declared (`dao.stream.md:879-882`). A future write outcome meaning \"effect unknown\" would be consumed as \"nothing appended, do not retry\" — wrong on both halves. The fix is one clause: the unrecognized-outcome default for **writes** becomes \"whether anything was appended is unknown\" (reads keep \"nothing observed\"). I adopt codex's correction.\n    30\t3. **Deferring OD-1 entirely — I disagree, on code evidence.** Consumers throw today, not just in prose: `fail` is `(throw (ex-info …))` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:41-43`), and `handle-put`/`handle-next` route every unrecognized outcome into it (`engine.cljc:192-195`, `245-254`; the doc's record at `dao.stream.md:818-821`). Serve relays outcome maps verbatim (findings.md:103), so a source on a newer contract makes this a **remotely triggered crash** the moment serve exists. The right sequence is accept-with-the-corrected-clause, not revise-indefinitely — and note the recursion: codex's own stricter alternative for the proxy (a truthful \"fetching\" answer instead of blocked-on-miss) would require a **new outcome**, which old consumers survive only under OD-1's rule. OD-1 acceptance is the prerequisite for the stricter design, not an alternative to it.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Synthesis position (CHANGE):** accept OD-3(a)+(2) and OD-2 as drafted; accept OD-1 with its write-effect clause corrected per codex; add one declarative paragraph to serve §4 naming the proxy's cached-read nature (see claim (a) below). Residual disagreement with codex: OD-1 acceptance timing — revise-then-accept vs accept-with-revision (same end state under the OD process, `dao.stream.md:796-801`).\n    33\t\n    34\t### The two directed verifications\n    35\t\n    36\t**(a) Codex's proxy-read-truth claim — PARTLY TRUE.**\n    37\t- *True as description:* the proxy answers `blocked` on a window miss regardless of what the source actually holds — the design says so itself (\"`blocked` also for *not here yet*\", findings.md:142; miss → demand → `blocked`, findings.md:137) — and `cursor` returns the last-observed anchors, not the current tail (findings.md:125-127).\n    38\t- *Not established: contract violation.* The IO model governs a handle that cannot yet know: \"an operation that cannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is called, and the eventual answer arrives… as data\" (`dao.stream.md:153-158`; also 81-82). The stale-`:newest` argument holds and is test-covered: stale is *earlier*, so it can only re-see, never skip (findings.md:127, criterion 3 at 290), a stale-`:oldest` degrades to the source's own `gap` with recovery cursor, and the proxy \"never produces a `gap` the source did not\" (findings.md:154). Both readings of `blocked`'s table row (`dao.stream.md:545`) are defensible — the text underdetermines it. That is a drafting gap, not a design flaw: the fix is the declaration paragraph (a transport declaring its outcome production reasons is already the contract's mechanism, `dao.stream.md:446-453`), not a redesign, and it does not touch the OD dispositions.\n    39\t- Verdict: behavior TRUE; \"OD-1–3 do not make those answers satisfy the contract\" PARTLY — the ODs were never claimed to; the missing piece is declarative and small.\n    40\t\n    41\t**(b) My claims — TRUE, now verified in code as well as docs.** Verbatim relay: findings.md:103 (\"Outcome maps cross **verbatim**, including the cursors inside them\"). Throwing consumers: `engine.cljc:41-43` (`fail` throws), `:192-195` (`handle-put` default → fail), `:245-254` (`handle-next` default → fail); doc record at `dao.stream.md:818-821`. `dao.lease` implemented: `src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc` — fact vocabulary, judge, holder disciplines, `make-judge`/`make-holder` composition constructors (86 defns).\n    42\t\n    43\t## Q6 — AGREE (positions converge; I adopt codex's two specifications)\n    44\t\n    45\tBoth round-1 answers were lease-governed; the deltas were phasing and shape. Codex's \"once grant, renewal, and **pre-grant lifetime** are specified\" names exactly what my \"composition-retired stopgap\" was — the entry's lifetime before any lease exists is composition-governed; that is now stated as a specified phase rather than an apology. I adopt both of codex's additions, verified sound: **(i)** pre-grant lifetime = composition retirement until the grant exists; **(ii)** \"a renewal sent through a proxy counts only when its required outcome is established under Q1\" — correct and load-bearing: a renewal is an append returning `ok` (`dao.lease.md:207-209`), and through a proxy `ok` is outbound acceptance, so the renewal counts only on the observed source outcome — Q1's amended rule applied to lease facts; without it a false renewal could be counted from an append the source refused. Also keep: the lease must not govern source retention (`dao.lease.md:94-95` — unserving gates attachment, not retention); the judge sits on the serving host (`dao.lease.md:112-117`); the machinery already exists (`dao.lease.cljc`); and `dao.lease.md:286-289` must move from `:ws/closed` to `:serve/close` with `:serve/reason` (design's own flag, findings.md:328). Remaining open for the spec: holder attribution for a remote resumer in v1 (composition-assigned holder id until channel auth exists — ties to Q3's tripwire).\n    46\t\n    47\t---\n    48\t\n    49\t## Final table\n    50\t\n    51\t| Q | FINAL position | agrees with codex | what remains open |\n    52\t|---|---|---|---|\n    53\t| Q1 | As designed; amend UCF §7.4.3 to resume `:put` on the observed source outcome; `append-unknown` leaves the wait undischarged | yes (agreed since r1) | Fable must write the `append-unknown` sentence into §7.4.3 |\n    54\t| Q2 | Defer held reads from v1; keep §3.7 additive | yes (agreed since r1) | none |\n    55\t| Q3 | Opaque composition-assigned (unguessable) ids in v1; duplicate-registration defined as relay policy; self-certifying additive later; tripwire: public relay ⇒ channel-auth + self-certifying stream identity, not registration-proof alone | **no (HOLD)** | codex's reply; the tripwire condition |\n    56\t| Q4 | Relay admission is composition-supplied policy, open default for trusted/dev deployments; link/peer lifecycle and caps part of the seam; postage a future policy, never protocol | **yes (CHANGE — adopted codex)** | none material |\n    57\t| Q5 | Accept OD-3(a)+(2) + OD-2 now; accept OD-1 **with** its write-effect default corrected to \"effect unknown\"; add the proxy cached-read declaration to serve §4 | partly (CHANGE/synthesis) | OD-1 acceptance timing (accept-with-revision vs revise-then-accept — same end state); the declaration paragraph's wording/home |\n    58\t| Q6 | Lease-governed via `dao.lease` over serve-table entries; pre-grant lifetime = composition retirement; renewal counts only on observed source `ok`; judge on the serving host; update `dao.lease.md:286-289` to `:serve/close` + reason | **yes (AGREE, adopted its additions)** | remote-holder attribution in v1 |\n    59\t| Q7 | `dao.stream.serve`; rename `dao.stream.serving` → copy-serving; fix ws prose 18-26 | yes (agreed since r1) | none |\n    60\t\n    61\tCONSENSUS: Q1, Q2, Q4, Q6, Q7\n    62\tOPEN: Q3, Q5\n    63\t\n    64\tNo concession was made to reach consensus: Q3 holds on the unauthenticated-identity evidence (`dao.stream.md:1087-1092`); Q5 moved because codex's OD-1 write-effect finding is genuinely correct, not as a trade.\n    65\t\n    66\tRead-only constraint honored: no repository file was edited. Round-2 working notes are appended to `~/.claude-glm/plans/read-collab-1790337700000-architect-dao-serene-shannon.md`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
