{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790337900000-architect-dao-stream-serve-q1-q7-mob-r2.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 12:12:24 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 19:12:24 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\t**Q3 — CHANGE.** Adopt GLM’s **opaque composition-assigned strings for v1**, confined to trusted deployments, and specify what a relay does with a duplicate `:serve/register`. A public relay needs proof that the registrant controls its claimed peer identity and an authenticated channel; signing registration alone does not protect later UDP traffic. GLM is right that UDP security is deferred, but its claim that every frame must be individually signed is too strong for an authenticated channel. The cited discovery text describes *stream* kickoff identities, not an existing peer-registration protocol. [Fable design:226](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:226), [Fable design:284](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:284), [dao.stream.discovery.md:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:70)\n     5\t\n     6\t**Q4 — HOLD.** Keep composition-supplied relay admission, allowing open admission when the deployment explicitly trusts its peers. GLM correctly identifies missing link cleanup and capacity bounds; those are needed under either policy. Its cited no-gating rule governs **DaoStream attachment**, while `register` and `dial` allocate relay routing state. Accepted postage for discovery deposits does not yet specify relay admission. [dao.stream.md:285](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:285), [dao.stream.discovery.md:159](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:159), [dao.stream.discovery.md:231](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:231), [Fable design:226](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:226)\n     7\t\n     8\t**Q5 — CHANGE.** Accept OD-3 (a)+(2) and OD-2. Adopt OD-1’s goal **now**, but revise its proposed fallback before acceptance: an unknown write outcome permits *no inference that nothing appended*. Do not automatically retry it. This addresses GLM’s real compatibility concern without contradicting OD-2’s unknown-effect case. The proxy’s read and anchor semantics remain a separate implementation gate. [dao.stream.md:100](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:100), [dao.stream.md:836](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:836), [dao.stream.md:863](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:863)\n     9\t\n    10\t**Q6 — AGREE on phasing; keep the lease shape open.** Decide on lease-governed served entries, permit composition retirement with an honest `not-found` in the serve-core v1, and integrate the lease next. GLM verified a real implementation, but its constructors do not decide who holds each served-entry grant, how the entry is kept before a grant, or how successive resumers take over. The UCF migration acceptance must still establish an adequate lifetime before claiming a reachable facade. A served-entry lease must remain separate from source retention and from UCF custody; proxy outbound `ok` alone cannot count as a source renewal. [dao.lease.md:94](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:94), [dao.lease.md:202](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:202), [dao.lease.md:229](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:229), [dao.lease implementation:2114](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc:2114), [dao.lease implementation:2215](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc:2215), [UCF:521](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:521)\n    11\t\n    12\t**Q5 claim checks**\n    13\t\n    14\t- **(a) TRUE.** The proposal explicitly returns `blocked` on any proxy cache miss and returns its last observed anchors. The contract says `blocked` means nothing at that source position yet, `gap` means eviction, and `:newest` is after the newest appended value. OD-1/2/3 address other questions; none changes those meanings. A stale anchor may cause rereading rather than skipping, but it is still not the requested current `:newest`. [Fable design:125](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:125), [Fable design:137](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:137), [dao.stream.md:477](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:477), [dao.stream.md:542](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:542)\n    15\t\n    16\t- **(b) PARTLY TRUE.** Source outcome maps do cross verbatim, both VM handlers throw on an unrecognized outcome, and `dao.lease` has `judge-step`, `make-judge`, and `make-holder`. Those facts make **an unknown-outcome policy** important. They do not establish that OD-1’s exact “nothing appended” fallback is safe for a future write outcome. [Fable design:103](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:103), [engine.cljc:179](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:179), [engine.cljc:216](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:216), [dao.lease implementation:1433](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc:1433), [dao.stream.md:836](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:836)\n    17\t\n    18\t| Question | FINAL position | Agrees with GLM | What remains open |\n    19\t|---|---|---|---|\n    20\t| Q1 | Outbound `ok`; resume served `:put` on correlated source `ok` | yes | Specify unknown-effect handling across migration |\n    21\t| Q2 | Defer held reads from v1 | yes | Performance policy later |\n    22\t| Q3 | Opaque IDs in trusted v1; authenticated binding for public use | yes | Duplicate-registration rule and public binding |\n    23\t| Q4 | Composition admission policy; open only in trusted deployments | no | Public relay policy and bounds |\n    24\t| Q5 | OD-2 and OD-3 now; revise OD-1; repair proxy read semantics | no | Safe OD-1 text and truthful proxy outcomes |\n    25\t| Q6 | Lease-governed, staged after serve-core v1 | yes | Grant, holder, handoff, and pre-grant lifetime |\n    26\t| Q7 | `dao.stream.serve`; rename existing copy-serving layer | yes | Correct WebSocket prose with rename |\n    27\t\n    28\tCONSENSUS: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q6, Q7; OPEN: Q4, Q5.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '123,154p;280,284p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '123,154p;280,284p'\"","aggregated_output":"   123\t### 3.6 Anchors across a hop\n   124\t\n   125\t`cursor` has no `blocked` outcome, yet a proxy cannot consult the source synchronously. The design does not invent a proxy-namespaced cursor (that would put a cursor in circulation that the source's own handle rejects, violating \"any handle on the same logical stream accepts it\"). Instead the server **piggybacks fresh `:oldest` and `:newest` cursors on `:serve/opened` and on every response**, and the proxy's `cursor` answers with the most recently observed one.\n   126\t\n   127\tThis is honest under the contract's own definitions. A stale `:newest` is *earlier* than a fresh one, so a composition that mints `:newest` before invoking an operation still observes everything the operation causes; it may additionally observe a few values appended between the last exchange and the mint, which is re-seeing, never skipping. A stale `:oldest` may point at a position since evicted, and the contract already says a fresh `:oldest` is a position rather than a completeness claim: the reader receives `gap` with the recovery cursor. Before the first `:serve/opened` the proxy holds no anchors and `cursor` answers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true` (OD-1's proposed key); the composition observes `:serve/opened` on the proxy's event medium and mints then.\n   128\t\n   129\t### 3.7 Held reads (optional, v1 decision needed)\n   130\t\n   131\tAcross a network a `blocked` answer costs a round trip. A `next` request may carry `:serve/hold true`; a server that honors it records the demand in session state and, on each of its own driver steps, re-polls the source, answering when the outcome is no longer `blocked` or when its hold budget (a constructor policy in the driver's clock domain) elapses, in which case it answers the `blocked` it has. No operation waits: the session holds state, the step returns. A proxy must tolerate a server that ignores the flag. Whether v1 includes this is an owner decision (Q2).\n   132\t\n   133\t## 4. The proxy handle: declared nature\n   134\t\n   135\tA proxy is a handle minted by `attach!` on a serve descriptor. It declares exactly the surface the `:serve/opened` frame reports, which is the source's declared surface; until then it declares the surface the descriptor's `:serve/surface` hint names, and a mismatch at open is a session failure deposited as data. `descriptor` returns the serve descriptor and the identity. The proxy is composed as three explicit pieces: the handle (an object over an explicit state atom, as `WsHandle` is), an **event medium** the composition wires and the channel adapter deposits into, and `proxy-step`, which the composition's driver calls with `now`.\n   136\t\n   137\tThe handle's `next` is a read of the proxy's **window**, a bounded host-local cache keyed by cursor value (structural equality; every cursor a reader holds was minted by the source, through this proxy or another). A miss records a **demand** in proxy state and answers `blocked`. `proxy-step` issues demands as requests, installs responses into the window, opens sessions, retries by the composition's policy, and evicts the window oldest-first. The window is a cache, not retention: an evicted window entry is re-fetched, and only the source ever answers `gap`.\n   138\t\n   139\t| Op        | Produces                                                                 | Excluded, and why |\n   140\t|-----------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n   141\t| `cursor`  | `ok` (last observed anchor, §3.6), `invalid-anchor` (relayed), `closed`, `transport-error` (`:establishing`, retryable; or channel failure) | — |\n   142\t| `next`    | every source outcome, relayed verbatim; `blocked` also for *not here yet*; `end` for positions beyond the window after the session ended; `cursor-mismatch` locally by identity; `invalid-cursor` for a non-map; `transport-error` for `:oversize` or channel failure | — |\n   143\t| `append!` | `ok` (accepted into the session's outbound path, carrying `:dao.stream.serve/id`), `full` (one append already outstanding, or channel outbound full; transient), `invalid-value` (outside the channel's domain), `closed`, `transport-error` | — |\n   144\t| `close!`  | `ok`                                                                    | — |\n   145\t| `descriptor` | `ok`                                                                 | — |\n   146\t\n   147\tThe proxy's writer surface is on the **session's ordered outbound path**, as ws's is on the connection's; the source's answer to an append arrives on the proxy's event medium as `{:serve/event :serve/appended :dao.stream.serve/id n :serve/outcome <map>}`. This is the contract's Writing rule and the ws precedent, and it has a cost for the UCF (Q1).\n   148\t\n   149\tSession loss is detachment, not ending: the proxy deposits `:serve/detached`, `append!` answers `closed`, and `next` answers what the window holds and then `end`, exactly as the contract specifies for a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream, so the reader's kept cursor survives; a fresh `attach!` with the same descriptor resumes at it. Re-establishing a lost session under the same proxy handle, answering `blocked` meanwhile, is a **proxy policy the composition supplies as data** (`:reconnect {:attempts n :after-ms t}`); the default is none, so nothing retries invisibly.\n   150\t\n   151\tTwo subtleties the contract forces:\n   152\t\n   153\t- `end` from the source is relayed. `end` from session loss is local. Both mean \"what this handle is on is closed\" and both are correct; a program that reads `end` as *the stream ended* will misread a channel loss unless its composition reconnects. This is the same limit ws documents under *Ending a served stream*, and the same answer: the serve `:serve/close` frame from the server carries `:serve/reason :ended` when the source's owner closed it, so the proxy can deposit `:serve/ended` versus `:serve/detached` and a composition can tell which it saw.\n   154\t- A proxy is not a retaining medium and has no `gap` of its own. It never produces a `gap` the source did not.\n   280\t## 12. What is contract, transport-owned and deferred\n   281\t\n   282\t- **The contract requires** (and this design depends on): OD-3 resolutions (a) and (2), cursors as plain data that survive serialization; OD-2's payload-correlation rule; OD-1's unrecognized-outcome rule with `:dao.stream/retry?`. None of the three is accepted yet (see C3).\n   283\t- **Transport-owned by this layer:** the frame vocabulary, session identity, the one-outstanding-append rule, anchor piggybacking, held reads, candidate order, retry and reconnect policies, relay and rendezvous state.\n   284\t- **Deliberately deferred:** UDP application fragmentation; parallel candidate trial; WebRTC DataChannel; authorization and postage for `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial` (a relay is a spam surface, and the contract's no-gating stance plus `dao.stream.discovery`'s postage note apply); self-certifying peer ids (v1 peer ids are opaque strings the composition assigns; the discovery document's kickoff-hash identities slot in as an additive rule); a lease over served entries; encryption of the channel (WSS is the host's; UDP has none until a Noise-shaped channel exists, and until then UDP serving is for trusted networks, as the DHT already says).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '421,453p;473,495p;536,550p;811,849p;879,890p;1080,1096p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '421,453p;473,495p;536,550p;811,849p;879,890p;1080,1096p'\"","aggregated_output":"   421\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   422\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   423\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   424\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   425\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   426\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   427\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   428\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   429\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   430\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   431\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   432\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n   433\tconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\n   434\tspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n   435\t\n   436\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   437\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   438\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   439\taction a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\n   440\tabout any event — it is the same action for every cause. One corner is\n   441\tirreducible: when the deposit that failed was the boundary's only channel, the\n   442\trecord of its own departure has nowhere to land, and the failure is observable\n   443\tonly to whoever holds the deposit result and through subsequent operations on\n   444\tthe handle.\n   445\t\n   446\tA transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it\n   447\texcludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is\n   448\timpossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n   449\treports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n   450\tevicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a `gap` to a cursor that\n   451\tspans the eviction, and never as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n   452\tthat cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\n   453\texclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\n   473\t## Cursors\n   474\t\n   475\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   476\t\n   477\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   478\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   479\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   480\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   481\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   482\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   483\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   484\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   485\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   486\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   487\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   488\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   489\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   490\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   491\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   492\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   493\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   494\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   495\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   536\t## Reading\n   537\t\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   811\t### OD-1. An unrecognized outcome has no defined meaning\n   812\t\n   813\t**The assumption.** The Result Convention makes each operation's outcome set\n   814\texhaustive and makes result *maps* open. The two rules are asymmetric: a future\n   815\textension may add a key, never an outcome. The contract assumes the seven\n   816\toutcome sets are complete for every transport that will ever exist.\n   817\t\n   818\t**Evidence.** Consumers are already total over the closed sets and treat\n   819\tanything else as a fault: `dao.stream.observe/step` classifies an unrecognized\n   820\tread or effect outcome as `transport-error` and reports `:defect` or `:failed`;\n   821\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-put` and `handle-next` throw. *Envelopes* declines to\n   822\treserve an outcome for gating and says gating \"arrives as an addition\", but\n   823\tunder the exhaustive rule a new outcome is not an addition.\n   824\t\n   825\t**What breaks.** Any condition the present outcomes cannot express honestly:\n   826\tattachment or append refused for lack of authority; a descriptor that is valid\n   827\tbut served elsewhere now; a transport that is throttling rather than full; an\n   828\tappend whose effect is unknown (OD-2). Each must either be squeezed into an\n   829\texisting outcome that misdescribes it (`not-found` for unauthorized, `full` for\n   830\tthrottled) or break every consumer.\n   831\t\n   832\t**Proposed resolution.** Keep each outcome set exhaustive *for this version of\n   833\tthe contract* and add one rule to the Result Convention that makes later\n   834\toutcomes safe to introduce:\n   835\t\n   836\t> A consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   837\t> `ok`; nothing observed and nothing appended; and not retryable, unless the\n   838\t> result map carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an\n   839\t> unrecognized outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's\n   840\t> assembly.\n   841\t\n   842\tWith that rule a newer transport degrades to \"refused, do not retry\" on an\n   843\tolder consumer instead of to an exception, and `:dao.stream/retry?` lets it\n   844\tsay when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\n   845\ttheir meanings and need not carry the key.\n   846\t\n   847\t**Cost if deferred.** Every consumer written in the meantime hard-codes\n   848\t\"unknown means defect\", and the first new outcome is a coordinated change\n   849\tacross all of them.\n   879\t1. Reword `transport-error` on `append!`: \"The transport failed to perform the\n   880\t   append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport\n   881\t   declares that its failures are clean.\" The three existing transports declare\n   882\t   clean failure, so nothing changes for them.\n   883\t2. State the rule the RPC layer already follows: **deduplication and\n   884\t   correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must retry safely puts an\n   885\t   identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice deduplicates on it.\n   886\t   The stream never promises exactly-once.\n   887\t3. Name where later answers land: a transport whose acceptance is not its\n   888\t   final answer (durability reached, far-end refusal) declares the channel on\n   889\t   which that answer is deposited, as *Close* already requires for a failing\n   890\t   flush.\n  1080\t\n  1081\t- **A writer does not learn where its value landed.** `append!`'s `ok` carries\n  1082\t  no cursor. Read-your-writes and causal references between streams arrive as\n  1083\t  an optional `:dao.stream/cursor` key on `ok`. Conditional append (\"only if\n  1084\t  the tail is still here\") would be an eighth operation; the design does not\n  1085\t  need it because it assumes **one writer per logical stream**, which\n  1086\t  `dao.space` practices and this document should say once in *Concurrency*.\n  1087\t- **History is not verifiable.** Identity is an opaque value nothing\n  1088\t  authenticates, and append-only is the transport's promise, not something a\n  1089\t  reader can check. Self-certifying identities and signed or hash-chained\n  1090\t  elements live in the payload, above the stream. The obligation on this layer\n  1091\t  is only the one it already meets: identity is compared structurally and never\n  1092\t  parsed.\n  1093\t- **Readiness.** Polling is O(n) in streams observed (*What it costs*). The\n  1094\t  extension is reserved, and `dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md` pays\n  1095\t  the cost in the runtime without it.\n  1096\t- **Granularity.** An element is a whole value; there is no byte offset within\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Q3 and Q4 match in substance once the trusted-v1 scope is explicit. On Q5, I agree to accept OD-1 with its write-effect clause corrected in the same decision. The remaining split is whether a declaration in serve §4 can make cached `blocked` and stale anchors satisfy the existing reader contract."}}
