{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '794,810p;100,112p;153,158p'\"","aggregated_output":"   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   794\t## Open Decisions\n   795\t\n   796\tStatus: **proposed, binding nothing.** Every other section of this document is\n   797\tcontract; this one is not. Each entry names an assumption the contract makes\n   798\twithout stating it, the use that would break it, and a proposed resolution.\n   799\tAn entry leaves this section in one of two ways: accepted, its text moves into\n   800\tthe section it amends and the entry is deleted; or rejected, the entry is\n   801\treplaced by one sentence in *Explicitly Absent* recording why. Drafted\n   802\t2026-09-19 from a review of this document against `dao.stream`, its three\n   803\ttransports (`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`), and their consumers.\n   804\t\n   805\tOD-1 to OD-4 are ordered by cost of delay. Each is a paragraph today, while\n   806\tthree transports and a handful of consumers exist, and a migration once a\n   807\tdurable or replicated transport ships. OD-5 was added afterwards and is\n   808\tnumbered by arrival; it concerns the layers above this contract rather than the\n   809\tcontract's own operations.\n   810\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"The Q5 disagreement is substantive. The contract defines `blocked` as nothing at that position yet and `:newest` as after the newest appended value; the proposal uses `blocked` for a cache miss and returns a previously observed anchor. I’ll report that split without delaying the OD decisions."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 12:20:12 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 19:20:12 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Q3 — CONFIRMED.** Both positions choose opaque, composition-assigned peer IDs for trusted v1 and require a defined duplicate-registration rule. Public deployment remains a separate authentication decision. A peer ID must remain distinct from the logical-stream identity, which the current contract does not authenticate. [Fable design:226](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:226), [Fable design:284](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:284), [dao.stream.md:1087](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:1087)\n\n**Q4 — CONFIRMED.** Both positions make relay admission composition-supplied, allow open admission in explicitly trusted deployments, require bounded peer/link state, and leave postage as a possible later policy. The contract’s attachment rule does not decide relay-control admission. [dao.stream.md:285](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:285), [Fable design:226](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:226)\n\n**Q5 — DIFFERS on (i); confirmed on (ii).**\n\n- **(i) Serve design gate:** A §4 declaration alone cannot make `blocked` on every cache miss mean “nothing at this position yet,” or make a previously observed anchor the current `:newest`. The proxy promises access to the original positioned sequence, while its window is expressly a cache. This needs a contract-consistent resolution before the serve design or implementation is accepted. It **does not gate acceptance of OD-1/2/3**, which answer separate contract questions. [dao.stream.md:421](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:421), [dao.stream.md:477](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:477), [dao.stream.md:542](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:542), [Fable design:125](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:125), [Fable design:137](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:137)\n- **(ii) OD-1 timing:** Accept **the revised rule now**, in the same decision that corrects its write fallback to “effect unknown; no automatic retry.” “Revise then accept” and “accept with revision” have the same required end state. The draft’s “nothing appended” conflicts with OD-2. [dao.stream.md:796](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:796), [dao.stream.md:836](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:836), [dao.stream.md:879](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:879)\n\nCONSENSUS: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q6, Q7; OPEN: Q5"}}
